Z||000006||daved||06-16-2000||10:26 PM||||John,  I think you should state your problem again, as you have not said what the actual fault is.<BR>From your posts, it appears as if you have a major power failure to your fuse panel. This will usually be the Fusible Link "A", the sort of fat part of the wire about 1 1/2" long # 2 Red, at the battery terminal box. A short in any of the red power wires (there are 8 of them!) will have blown the fusible link.<P>You do know that you cannot measure the power circuits to ground using an Ohmmeter, as any power consuming device (lights, motors, relays etc.)will have a certain resistance to ground.<BR>Follow your main red wire (the one from the fusible link) till you reach a multiple point splice (#S206)with 7 other red wires. Reconnect your battery, and measure Volts to ground from this point. If you have 12Vdc or better, then all is well. If 0 Volts, Link is a gonner!<BR>See Haynes page 271 for the power distribution diagram.<BR>Let us know how you get on, Dave||||reg||1
Z||000007||johnmac||06-17-2000||07:28 AM||||I checked my Fusible Link A again. It is okay. I am getting power.<P>THE PROBLEM STILL IS <P>The 8 red power wires all indicate continuity to ground per the meter. When I connect the power wire (as mentioned above in my first message - it is isolated at present) the entire power circuit indicates a grounded connection.<P>I have pulled almost all the wiring harness apart down the middle of the car with not solution.<P>IS THERE A PRESSURE SENSOR WHICH IF NOT WORKING CAN GROUND OUT THESE POWER WIRES?<P>ANY IDEALS - - I AM GETTING DESPERATE!!!<P>John MacHaffie||||reg||8
Z||000008||Mark||06-17-2000||09:38 AM||||John,<P>If you really did have a fault to ground, (and a charged battery) your fusible link should have opened up, otherwise your car would be on fire!<P>Again, what is the problem? what doesn't work? <P>If you are not getting power to the fuse block, check continuity (resistance) from the battery + terminal to the fuse block.<P>If that chacks OK, then check for continuity from the battery minus terminal to ground.  You may have a bad ground connection ( I'm guessing something (or everything) electrical is not working).||||reg||1
Z||000009||johnmac||06-17-2000||03:44 PM||||Per the ground<BR>I am rebuilding my 86 GT and put in all new ground wires. Ground at the battery side should not be a problem.<P>I do get a full voltage reading pass the Fusible Link A.<P>Everything is working except for the temperature gauge wheras a new sender unit should take care of that.<P>Problem is again my power ciruit shows continuity to ground. I thought the negatives and positives are suppose to be separate.<P>I put a meter on the red power wires at the fuse block and they show continuity to ground.<P>This is not right - or is my basic knowledge of electricity wrong.<P>My concern is that this may interfere with the ECM.<P>HELP!<P>John<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||mbramble||06-17-2000||10:50 PM||||Is there continuity to ground (a normal indication)--- or is there a short circuit (0 ohms) to ground (now that's a problem)??  A SHORT CIRCUIT is not the same as 'continuity'. <P>If you touch the two meter probes together, that is a SHORT CIRCUIT  -- 0 OHMS.  IS that what you see when you measure between the red wire and ground???  If so, you have a SHORT and will need to trace it out.<P>However, there WILL be continuity unless you remove every bulb, sensor, and anything else that is connected to ground.  Voltage flows from the battery, thru the wiring, thru the bulbs/sensors/etc., to ground (some will say it actually flows the other way) -- that's what make these things work.  Each of those components have some resistance but will show 'continuity' to ground.  And when you consider that many of them have relatively low resistance and are in parallel with others that have low resistance, you can actually see a  low resistance between the hot wire(s) and ground -- but not a SHORT CIRCUIT to ground (0 ohms).<P>I don't know about how the wiring is but it is mentioned above that a single red wire feeds 8 other red wires.  You indicate that there is no "short" to the red wire, on the battery side, once you disconnet it from the 8 other red wires.  That would make pretty good sence as there are probably no componenets (bulbs, sensors, etc.) between the battery and where you broke the wire.  If there is a SHORT CIRCUIT at the group of 8 wires, you will have to separate them and see which has the SHORT CIRCUIT.  Then, you can go point-to-point following that wire to find out where the SHORT CIRCUIT is.<P>But, if there is no SHORT CIRCUIT (0 ohms), only a low resistance (or continuity) and everything works fine, and you aren't blowing fuses, it doesn't seem to me that you have a problem.....<P>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 06-17-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 06-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||johnmac||06-18-2000||07:28 AM||||Thank you for the clarification. <P>This appears to answer my MISSION IMPOSSIBLE which has been bugging me for some time.<P>Please excuse my lack of knowledge on this subject and you Fiero guys are great.<P>Thanks again,<P>John MacHaffie||||reg||10
Z||000012||prof bobo||06-19-2000||03:54 PM||||Hey folks, don't get caught in the trap of 'cold' resistance vs 'hot' resistance.  In other words, components like light bulbs can appear to be 'shorts' (0 ohms) when they are cold (not lit, not drawing current).  Their resistance only shows up when they can draw their rated current and light up -the hot glowing filament is a testament to the resistance and current flow!  Relay coils and  semiconductor devices (diodes, transistors, etc) can also fool you.  The bottom line is, if you suspect a problem in a bus, set your meter up to indicate a 'short', then start disconnecting everything on that bus until the short goes away.  Consider carefully the nature of the components you disconnect, if it was a bulb or a relay then you never really had a short in the first place, other components might require some other testing to determine if they are actually shorted.  Of course, if after you've disconnected everything on the circuit, the short remains, then you've probably got cut or abraded insulation and you'll need to apply some visual inspection -and perhaps some easily repairable strategically placed cuts to isolate the area.||||reg||1
Z||000013||lowCG||06-19-2000||06:39 PM||||A REALLY expensive meter will show some of these things also,but usually not worth several hundred dollars.<BR>When you re-connect the cicuit,I'd recommend soldering the connection to eliminate any capacitance or create any opportunity for corrosion with one of those crimp connectors.<BR>||||reg||1
